Revision history for OCP

{{$NEXT}}
- `OCP::Config::validate` now accepts `local` as a worker-pool
      provider and rejects unknown ones with a message that lists the
      valid types (karr #115). The check routes through
      `OCP::Provider->known_type` / `OCP::Provider->types`, so the
      worker list and the control-plane list share one source -- the
      fourth hand-kept `hetzner|ssh` regex is gone. New subtests in
      t/15-config-validation.t cover both directions.
    - `OCP::Drift::distribution_drift` no longer swallows API failures into
      "no drift" (karr #119). The bare `eval { ... } or return` collapsed every
      list('Node') failure -- revoked token, RBAC denial, TLS, apiserver 5xx --
      into a silent undef, so `ocp status` printed a green drift summary with
      no warning, no log, and no entry while the actual reason nothing was
      compared was that the API never answered. The sub now returns one
      `kind => 'error'` drift entry naming the distribution and the underlying
      exception, and carps the exception to stderr -- the same way
      OCP::Cmd::Status already handles its `list_nodes` failure. New subtests
      in t/25-drift.t assert the entry carries the original error text and
      reaches the drift list `detect()` returns, so a stray `ocp status`
      against a broken kubeconfig does not stay silent.
    - The RKE2/K3s join-token path now lives in one place
      (`$OCP::Node::K3S_TOKEN_PATH` / `$OCP::Node::RKE2_TOKEN_PATH`) and
      `OCP::Cmd::Node::Add` and `OCP::Cmd::Apply::CR` read it from there
      (karr #122). The two paths were spelled as literals in both files,
      and bumping one without the other meant `ocp node add` and
      `ocp apply` disagreed on which file to `cat` -- one came back with
      an empty join token and died. The constants sit alongside
      `$OCP::Node::READY_TIMEOUT` so the pattern matches the one karr
      #109/#112 settled on for the address and SSH budgets, and a new
      subtest in t/16-node.t asserts both call sites reference the
      constants instead of spelling the literals.
    - `ocp destroy` no longer strands a local-provider node running and
      billing (karr #116). The fallback node list hard-coded
      `$cp->{provider} // 'ssh'` and `$w->{provider} eq 'ssh'` -- the
      second silently dropped every local worker pool, the first relabelled
      a local CP as ssh; the delete loop's `elsif ($node->{provider} eq 'ssh')`
      branch then had no branch for `local`, so even a node that DID make
      the list (via `.ocp/status.yaml`) fell through with nothing while the
      teardown announced "Cluster destroyed.". The literals are replaced
      with `OCP::Provider->known_type(...)` checks, the same shape karr
      #103 settled on for six other input gates; ssh and local both consume
      `OCP::Role::Provider::ExistingHost`, so the delete call is identical,
      and only ssh still needs the cluster key (local IS the host). New
      subtests in t/77-destroy-local-provider.t cover the status.yaml path,
      both halves of the spec fallback, and a regression that fails if the
      `eq 'ssh'` and `// 'ssh'` literals come back.
    - `ocp destroy` now removes `.ocp/status.yaml` and `.ocp/deployed.yaml`
      even when the early-return path fires (karr #78). A project whose
      cluster was torn down out of band left both files describing a
      cluster that no longer existed; the next `ocp apply` then compared
      a fresh cluster against the hashes of one that was gone and
      announced every component as "up to date" against a registry that
      was never rolled out (ADR 0004). The cleanup now lives in one
      helper, called from both the main path and the "no nodes to
      destroy" return; `--keep_status` is still the opt-out and still
      means the same thing in both places. New subtests in
      t/47-deployed-state.t assert the files are gone on the
      empty-nodes/spec-stripped shape cortex was in.
    - The GPU ClusterPolicy now writes spec.cdi.enabled explicitly
      (karr #76). The #23 decision -- "OCP does not make the nvidia
      runtime the node's default runtime" -- rests on the operator
      writing NVIDIA_RUNTIME_SET_AS_DEFAULT=false into the toolkit env,
      and that write is gated on config.CDI.IsEnabled(). IsEnabled() was
      returning true only because the ClusterPolicy CRD's kubebuilder
      default fills the field in for nil -- an upstream default OCP
      pinned nothing to and tested nothing against. The field is now
      written in the spec (true), so the decision rides on the line OCP
      actually emits, and a future CRD-default flip in gpu-operator
      cannot silently make nvidia the defaultRuntimeName for every
      container on the node. New subtests in t/40-gpu-clusterpolicy.t
      assert the field is present and the rendered YAML contains the
      literal "enabled: true" under cdi:.
    - cert-manager's reconcile gate now compares the recorded version
      against OCP::Versions->get_component_version('cert_manager'), not
      against mere presence. The previous `if ($cm_running && $deployed->{certmanager})`
      matched every truthy entry, so a cert-manager version bump in
      OCP::Versions was silently skipped on the reconcile path -- only
      OCP::Drift, which reads the running image from the cluster, caught
      it. Registry, NFD and the GPU operator already used this equality
      shape; cert-manager is now consistent with them (karr #69).
    - The OCPNodeProvider CRD enum finally knows `local` (karr #110). The
      schema declared `spec.type: [hetzner, ssh]` while OCP::Provider had
      carried `local` since the day OCP::Provider::Local existed, so
      `ocp provider add --type local` cleared CLI validation and then
      failed at the API server with a 422 about a schema the operator
      never wrote. The CRD enum is now [hetzner, ssh, local]; a new
      subtest in t/75 reads the enum off disk and compares it against
      OCP::Provider->types so the next drift is caught at `make test`
      instead of at the cluster.
    - The address wait is one budget again. `$OCP::Node::ADDRESS_TIMEOUT` is
      gone: the constant now lives in `$OCP::Provider::Hetzner` (where the
      wait is owned, the same shape `OCP::SSH::WAIT_TIMEOUT` already
      uses), and the two callers — `OCP::Cmd::Apply::Bootstrap` for the
      control plane and `OCP::Node::_resolve_host` for every worker —
      take the module's default. Naming their own number was the
      asymmetry karr #109 just killed for SSH; the address wait had it
      too, with three 120s that would have drifted the moment one of
      them moved (karr #112). The single-source-of-truth test that
      pinned it is the same shape as the SSH one — a budget comparison,
      not a literal number per side.
    - `OCP::SSH::is_reachable` no longer takes a `$timeout` it never read.
      The parameter used to land in `$timeout //= 5`, then vanish — the
      actual ceiling was `connect_timeout` (10 s) on a single probe. A
      caller thinking "give it 10 s" got one probe with a 10 s
      ConnectTimeout, and was told its number mattered. Removed.
    - t/43-k8s-call-contract.t now derives the kind-bearing method list
      from Kubernetes::REST at test time (via a helper that reads the
      package source and picks out every top-level `sub NAME {`
      definition) instead of hard-coding `(get|delete|patch|list|ensure|
      _delete_object)` in the regex. The hand-kept list silently dropped
      methods every time Kubernetes::REST grew one -- patch_status in 1.107
      was the last known casualty -- so a fresh method was a fresh
      regression that the next karr ticket would have to repeat this
      fix for. The dynamic derivation also catches log/port_forward/exec/
      attach/watch and the object-shaped update/create/update_status/
      ensure_all/ensure_only, which the static list never did; OCP::Node's
      own _delete_object wrapper (added in #35) is still joined in by a
      small explicit list, since it is OCP code rather than Kubernetes::REST
      code. The regex's "what kind of literal counts as an api-version"
      half is unchanged -- broadening it caught ->scp_to('/local/file'),
      ->_parse_image_ref('ghcr.io/foo/bar:v1.2.3') and similar, so the
      `forbid method-name` shape stays. A regression subtest pins the
      dynamic derivation in place: if anyone re-hardcodes the alternation
      (or half-reverts and leaves the helper unused) the test fails on
      names Kubernetes::REST 1.107 already exposed.
    - dist.ini now sets `version_finder = :MainModule` + `:ExecFiles`
      so the single-source claim from 4aa92cf is true in the build,
      not just in the repo (karr #74). The Author::GETTY bundle
      reads `version_finder` from its own payload (lines 326-331 of
      PluginBundle/Author/GETTY) and forwards it to PkgVersion,
      RewriteVersion::Transitional and BumpVersionAfterRelease;
      without an explicit value the rewriters fall back to
      [':InstallModules', ':ExecFiles'] and PkgVersion quietly
      re-inserted `our $VERSION = '0.001';` into all 55 modules
      behind the user's back. With the finders restricted to
      lib/OCP.pm and the bin scripts, only lib/OCP.pm carries
      $VERSION in the source, bin/ocp and bin/robocop keep their
      anchor line for RewriteVersion to stamp, and the 55 modules
      no longer ship with stale version stamps. t/77 replaces the
      silent drift with a regression test.
    - t/51-pod.t now discovers its scope from globs instead of an
      enumeration. The hardcoded list of five Provider modules was
      silent about new ones and quietly drifted when Choices.pm
      joined the Weaver-scope set; the dynamic variant
      (lib/OCP/Provider*.pm, lib/OCP/Role/Provider*.pm, plus
      lib/OCP/Choices.pm as the named exception the original
      ticket called out) means a freshly added module joins the
      assertion set for free. Out-of-scope modules -- the other ~35
      -- are not asserted here; their migration is a separate
      ticket.
    - A `skip_if` in `@COMPONENT_PROBES` that names an `OCP::Config` method
      which does not exist used to make the probe silently run as if no
      skip_if had been set -- a cluster with `nocert: true` would then be
      reported as cert-manager-drifting after someone renamed or removed
      `OCP::Config::no_cert`. The table and the method live in the same
      distribution, so the name can only be wrong by accident; that is now a
      croak at the top of the probe loop, naming the offending target.
    - `ocp node add --role quatsch` is refused by OCP, not by Kubernetes. The
      role was never checked on the way out: it went into spec.role, where the
      OCPNode CRD declares `enum: [control-plane, worker]`, and the operator
      got a raw 422 about a schema they never wrote. It is now rejected before
      the first API call, naming the two roles.
    - Six more rejections now say what would have worked, in the same shape
      `ocp quatschkommando` has answered in since 0.001: an unknown node
      (`ocp node rm`, `ocp ssh --node`) lists the nodes that exist, an unknown
      `ocp keys show --name`/`--purpose` lists the keys and purposes in
      keys.yaml (names and purposes only — no key material, ever), an unknown
      provider type lists the types OCP can build, and `ocp update` on a
      version with no manifest says so. `ocp ssh` also stops calling a node
      unknown when the name was right and only its address is missing.
    - `ocp update --component TYPO` is no longer a silent no-op. The selector
      used to skip every component in the loop, leave `@updates` empty, and
      fall through to "All components up to date" with exit 0 — the same
      line, the same code, as a real nothing-to-do. It now refuses the way
      the other rejections in this release do, listing every component the
      current manifest has.
    - A worker that is slow to boot is no longer lost for good. Waiting for
      SSH on a freshly created machine had two budgets for one question: 120s
      when `ocp apply` bootstraps a control plane, 60s when a worker is
      installed — and on the worker side running out is terminal, so a server
      whose sshd needed 70 seconds was marked Failed permanently while it kept
      billing. Both now take the same 120s from `$OCP::SSH::WAIT_TIMEOUT`, the
      module that does the waiting; the control-plane path spends exactly what
      it always did. The wait had been unreachable for Hetzner workers until
      the address fix in this same release, which is why nobody had hit it.
    - `ocp node add` says what it is waiting for. It could sit silent for ten
      minutes and then report "did not reach Ready state" with an SSH-key
      hint, which is the wrong diagnosis for a machine that was merely still
      installing. It now prints each phase as the node reaches it, and
      distinguishes "ran out of time" from "failed" — nothing is rolled back,
      and re-running the command continues from the phase in the CR. The
      budget for that wait went from 600s to 900s and lives in one place
      (`$OCP::Node::READY_TIMEOUT`) instead of being restated by three
      callers: it has to cover the address wait, the SSH wait, the whole RKE2
      install and the join, and no longer did.
    - `ocp provider add --location nbg1` now actually puts servers in nbg1.
      `--location`, `--server-type` and `--image` were written onto the
      OCPNodeProvider CR and printed by `ocp provider ls`, and nothing ever
      read them back — the provider level was decoration and only the node
      level counted. They are now the middle rank of a stated order: a node's
      own `ocp node add --server-type` wins, then the provider's default, then
      the built-in default (cx32 / debian-13 / fsn1). An empty value counts as
      "not set" at every rank, so a provider that says nothing changes
      nothing, and a provider named `hetzner-nbg1` is finally worth having.
      The control plane is unaffected: `ocp apply` names all three out of
      ocp.yaml, which is the top rank. The CRD also declared
      `serverType: default: cx23` — a server type Hetzner does not sell — so
      every provider CR read back cx23 while every server came up cx32. That
      default is gone rather than corrected: a schema default is materialised
      into every CR by the API server, which would have made "this provider
      does not care" impossible to express. One `ocp apply` clears the stale
      value out of an existing CR.
    - `ocp destroy` no longer leaves paid Hetzner workers running. Every
      server carries an `ocp-cluster` label, and the teardown deletes by it —
      but the worker path (`ocp node add` and robocop alike) took the cluster
      name from the OCPNodeProvider CR's own name, which `ocp apply` writes as
      `hetzner-default`. So the control plane was labelled with the cluster
      name and every worker with `hetzner-default`: the teardown never saw
      them, they kept running and kept billing, and it still printed "Cluster
      destroyed". The same wrong label made the idempotency check miss its own
      machines, so a second provisioning attempt created a SECOND server
      instead of finding the first. The provider CR now carries
      `spec.clusterName`, written by `ocp apply` and `ocp provider add` and
      read back by the worker path, so both ends label identically. A CR
      without it is refused rather than used — one `ocp apply` rewrites it.
      Servers already running under the old label cannot be relabelled: `ocp
      destroy` now lists them by name, id and address at the end of a teardown
      and prints the `hcloud` selector to remove them by hand. It never
      deletes them itself — that label is generic enough to belong to another
      cluster in the same project.
    - A rejected provider name now says which providers exist, with their
      types. `--provider` on `ocp node add` and the NAME of `ocp provider rm`
      take the name of an OCPNodeProvider CR, but `ocp init --provider` and
      `spec.type` take a provider type, so `--provider ssh` for the CR
      `ssh-default` that `ocp apply` writes is an easy mistake — and the old
      "Provider 'ssh' not found" was a correct rejection that helped with
      nothing. Both commands now answer in the shape an unknown command word
      does: `Unknown provider 'ssh'.` followed by `Available: ssh-default
      (type ssh), hetzner-default (type hetzner)`, plus a line naming the
      confusion when the rejected name is a type. A cluster with no provider
      at all points at `ocp apply` and `ocp provider add` instead of an empty
      list, and `ocp node add --help` now shows an example. Provider types are
      still never resolved to a CR that carries them: names and types are
      separate namespaces and may collide.
    - Hetzner workers are no longer created without SSH keys. A worker
      provisioned through an OCPNode CR reached the Hetzner provider with the
      node spec rather than named options, and the provider read only the
      options: every such machine was created with an empty authorized_keys —
      running, billable and unreachable for OCP forever — and the
      --server-type/--location/--image given to `ocp node add` were dropped
      for the cx32/debian-13/fsn1 defaults. The provider now reads the spec
      too (a directly passed option still wins, so control-plane bootstrap is
      unchanged), and it refuses to create a server with no SSH key at all
      instead of leaving an unreachable machine behind. Which key that is
      comes from `spec.hetzner.sshKeyName` on the OCPNodeProvider CR, written
      by `ocp apply` and `ocp provider add` as the cluster's admin key name —
      the same key bootstrap uploads. Run `ocp apply` once to add the field to
      an existing cluster's provider, or set it with
      `ocp provider add --ssh-key-name`.
    - Secure mode has two SSH key tiers instead of three: robo
      (`purpose: automation`, no PIN2) for unattended automation, and admin
      (`purpose: admin`, age + PIN2) for everything a human triggers —
      bootstrap, update, node add, destroy and `ocp ssh`. The bootstrap key
      .ocp/id_ed25519 is gone from secure mode and lives on only in
      --nopassword dev mode, where there is no keys.yaml and it is the sole
      piece of key material. It was kept for `provider: ssh` on the theory
      that a pre-existing machine can only trust a key a human put there by
      hand; but the admin public key is printable with `ocp keys show
      --purpose admin`, so a human distributes exactly the key the Hetzner
      API uploads. The provider decides who distributes the key, never which
      key. Consequently `ocp init` writes no bootstrap key in secure mode on
      any provider, and its `provider: ssh` report now prints the ADMIN
      public key as the one to put in authorized_keys; `ocp ssh` picks its
      key the same way as every other command and no longer demands PIN2 in
      a dev-mode project that has no admin key to unlock (karr #94 dissolves
      with the premise); `--ssh-key` is reported as not applicable in secure
      mode rather than silently honoured. An existing .ocp/id_ed25519 is
      still never regenerated and never deleted — it is reported as legacy.
    - MIGRATION, `provider: ssh` clusters only: machines set up before this
      carry the BOOTSTRAP public key in authorized_keys and have never seen
      the admin one. Add the admin key on every machine — `ocp keys show
      --purpose admin`, appended to /root/.ssh/authorized_keys — BEFORE
      running any other ocp command against the cluster. Nothing verifies
      this in advance and there is deliberately no fallback to the old key;
      what OCP does instead is diagnose it, so `ocp apply`, `ocp ssh`,
      `ocp node add` and `ocp destroy` explain the lockout and name the two
      steps out of it when a connection is refused and a bootstrap key is
      still lying in the project. Hetzner clusters and dev-mode projects
      need nothing.
    - A WORKER that will not come up is now covered by that diagnosis too. It
      was the one machine left out: a worker's SSH happens inside OCP::Node,
      which is shared with the in-cluster robocop controller and therefore
      says nothing about `ocp keys show` — a command robocop does not have and
      whose output nobody would read. So a pre-existing ssh worker that still
      only trusts the bootstrap key failed with a bare timeout and no cause.
      The two CLI paths that drive OCP::Node — `ocp apply`'s worker rollout
      and `ocp node add` — now print the explanation themselves when a worker
      does not reach Ready and a bootstrap key is still in the project, once
      per run however many machines are unreachable. It stays a diagnosis
      after the fact: nothing checks in advance which key a machine accepts,
      nothing falls back to the bootstrap key, and a worker that did not come
      up still fails with the same exit code (karr #97).
    - `ocp destroy` no longer strands paid servers when it cannot get an SSH
      key. Its ssh-node branch needs the PIN2-protected admin key now, which
      means a lookup that can fail — a wrong PIN, no terminal, no keys.yaml.
      That lookup happens once, before the delete loop and in its own eval,
      and only when the node list actually contains an ssh machine, so a
      Hetzner-only teardown still asks for nothing. When it fails, the
      RKE2/K3s uninstall steps are skipped with a visible warning and the
      manual command named, while every Hetzner server is still deleted
      through the API. An unreachable host during the uninstall is also
      reported as a warning instead of being announced as a success.
    - `ocp update`, `ocp node add` and drift remediation work on a
      secure-mode Hetzner cluster (karr #87). All three reached for
      .ocp/id_ed25519 as the key to log in with, but a Hetzner machine was
      never given that key: OCP uploads the ADMIN public key through the
      provider API before the server exists, and since #85 `ocp init` does
      not create a bootstrap key there at all. So `ocp update` died with
      "cannot read SSH key", `ocp node add` could not fetch the join token,
      and the reconcile path's Rex remedy declined every drift it found —
      on the one provider and mode combination where it mattered. The
      question "which key does this cluster's machines trust" now has one
      answer, in the new OCP::ClusterKey. (It answered "the bootstrap key
      for a `provider: ssh` machine, the admin key otherwise" when this was
      written; the two-tier change above narrowed that to the mode alone.)
      `ocp update` and `ocp node add` therefore prompt
      for PIN2 once on a secure Hetzner project, where they previously
      failed; `ocp apply` against an existing cluster asks only if a drift
      repair actually has to run, so a clean reconcile stays as promptless
      as before, and `ocp apply --dry-run`, `ocp status` and drift detection
      never ask because they never open an SSH connection at all. Without a
      terminal — a piped or scheduled run — the admin key is refused with a
      message naming what it wanted, instead of blocking on an invisible
      password prompt. `ocp destroy` was left alone by #87 and is covered by
      the teardown entry above instead.
    - The decrypted admin key no longer outlives the command that needed
      it, and no longer disappears while it is still in use (karr #87). The
      temp file pair OCP wrote for it had no owner: one path created it with
      UNLINK => 0 and left a readable private key in /tmp, another tied it
      to a lexical that went out of scope when the sub returned — so on a
      secure Hetzner deploy the key was unlinked the moment
      bootstrap_control_plane returned and every worker afterwards failed
      as "Could not read join token from CP". The public half nobody
      unlinked at all. OCP::ClusterKey owns both files, ties them to the
      command object, and removes them when it goes away — including while
      Perl unwinds out of a die.
    - Worker installs no longer point Rex at a public key file that was
      never written (karr #93). OCP::Node wrote the private key to a temp
      file and handed Rex the path; OCP::Rex sets REX_PUBLIC_KEY to that
      path plus ".pub" unconditionally, and nothing put a file there — so
      every worker install, robocop's as much as `ocp node add`'s, ran with
      REX_PUBLIC_KEY naming something that did not exist. The same defect
      was fixed for the control plane by #87; the worker path was outside
      that ticket. Both halves are now written and owned together by the new
      OCP::TempKeyPair, which OCP::ClusterKey uses as well. The public half
      is read out of the private key material itself rather than passed in
      or shelled out for: robocop is handed private key material and nothing
      else, so anything only the CLI could supply would have left the
      controller broken, and `ssh-keygen -y` would have made openssh-client
      an undeclared runtime dependency of the controller image.
    - `ocp apply` no longer signs off with "All N component(s) up to date"
      after a drift repair it could not carry out. Detected drift whose
      remedy declined to run is now counted and named, and the run says it
      did not bring the cluster back to spec.
    - `ocp init` no longer destroys a project it was run in for the first
      time (karr #86). .ocp/ is gitignored while keys.yaml, secrets.yaml and
      age.key.enc are committed on purpose, so the second person to clone the
      repo had all the encrypted material and no .ocp/age.key. Init checked
      `has_age_key` — "is there one on this machine" — heard no, and
      generated a fresh keypair over .ocp/age.pub; keys.yaml was then bound
      to a recipient whose private half no longer existed anywhere, and init
      itself died with "Could not decrypt data key with any of the provided
      identities". Init now asks whether the PROJECT has a key, which
      age.key.enc and the plaintext recipient in the committed SOPS files
      answer without holding any key, and unlocks the existing one with PIN1
      instead of minting a new one. Unlocking also restores .ocp/age.pub from
      the private half, so a clone can encrypt again and not merely decrypt.
      Where there is no way in — no age.key.enc, or --nopassword against a
      secure project, or a wrong PIN1 — init aborts and names what is missing
      and where it comes from, writing nothing. The same protection sits in
      OCP::Secrets::generate_age_key itself, which now refuses to run while
      anything is bound to a recipient, so it holds for any other path to
      generation as well.
    - `ocp apply` in dev mode (`ocp init --nopassword`) no longer mints over a
      committed binding (karr #90). The dev-mode branch used the
      `has_age_key`-on-this-machine check `ocp init` had before #86, and once
      #86 put the safety net inside `OCP::Secrets::generate_age_key` that
      branch hit the generic "Unlock the existing key with PIN1 (age.key.enc)"
      croak — advice that has nowhere to go on a path without a PIN. The
      check is now `project_has_age_key` (added in #86): nothing committed,
      generate as before; project bound to a recipient and `.ocp/age.key`
      missing, abort with the dev-mode-specific answer (the key has to come
      back from a backup or from the machine that created the project —
      `.ocp/` is gitignored). Generating over an existing recipient still
      has to be impossible; OCP::Secrets::generate_age_key's own refusal is
      unchanged and now sits below a check that gives the user the right
      diagnosis for this branch.
    - `ocp init` creates the bootstrap key .ocp/id_ed25519 for
      `provider: ssh` in secure mode, not only under --nopassword (karr #85).
      OCP::Cmd::Apply::Bootstrap picks exactly that file when
      `$no_password_mode || $provider eq 'ssh'`, so a secure-mode SSH project
      had no key at all and `ocp apply` died with "SSH not reachable on HOST
      after 120s" — a network message for what was really "nothing to
      authenticate with". Init now creates it under that same condition, so
      the two halves agree. A secure Hetzner or local control plane still
      gets none: it is sent the admin key through the provider API and never
      reads this path, and writing an inert key there would only cost
      `ocp update`, `ocp destroy`, `ocp node add` and drift remediation their
      honest "the key is missing" failure. The key carries no PIN by design —
      the bootstrap has to read it without prompting. An existing key is
      never regenerated and never silently replaced, whatever the provider,
      since its public half is already in authorized_keys on a running
      cluster's machines. Switching ocp.yaml to `provider: ssh` and re-running
      `ocp init` picks the key up without repeating --provider. Secure-mode
      `ocp init --provider ssh` also prints the public key to distribute,
      which it previously left out entirely because the only file it knew how
      to print did not exist.
    - `ocp init --ssh-key` is evaluated in secure mode (karr #85). It was
      declared, documented, and read only on the --nopassword branch, so in
      secure mode it was accepted and silently dropped. Where it cannot be
      honoured it now says so instead: against an existing bootstrap key
      already in service it names --force rather than overwriting a key that
      machines already trust, and against a provider that has no bootstrap
      key it reports that the flag does not apply.
    - New `ocp keys show [--purpose admin|--name NAME]` prints the public
      half of a key in keys.yaml (karr #84). After `ocp init` there was no
      way to see the admin public key, which is what `ocp node add` and
      manual distribution both need. It asks for PIN1 at most: the public
      field sits behind the age layer only, the PIN2 layer covers `private`.
      Only the key goes to stdout, so it pipes into authorized_keys.
    - OCP::Keys::decrypt_all_to_disk is removed (karr #84). It had no caller
      anywhere in lib/ or bin/, but its POD promised a .ocp/keys/ layout
      that documentation and deploy plans then assumed existed. As a way to
      read a public key it was also the wrong shape — it wrote every
      *private* key to disk in plaintext as a side effect. OCP::Keys' POD no
      longer shows `public:` as plaintext in keys.yaml either (every leaf is
      age-encrypted) and now states which field each layer actually covers.
    - Status writes work again against Kubernetes::REST 1.107, which is now
      the required version (together with IO::K8s 1.107). 1.107 is the first
      release with a native `patch_status`, and OCP preferred a native writer
      over its own hand-built one as soon as the client offered it — but
      forwarded its flat kind/name/status arguments, which that method does
      not accept. Every status write then died with "Invalid arguments to
      patch_status()": worker provisioning could not leave phase=Pending and
      `ocp apply` could not stamp the control-plane CR. The call now uses the
      client's own form.
    - An unknown subcommand now fails instead of quietly resolving to the
      root (karr #67). `ocp quatschkommando --help` printed the root usage
      and exited 0; MooX::Cmd looks for the first word it recognises
      anywhere in the argument vector and lets the rest fall on the floor,
      so a command a given build does not have was indistinguishable from
      success. Every command word is now checked against its own level
      before dispatch, the error names the commands that do exist, and the
      exit code is non-zero. Three variants of the same hole closed with
      it: `ocp typo apply` used to run apply, `ocp node typo --help`
      returned 0, and an alias behind an option (`ocp -v deploy-image`)
      was not resolved at all.
    - `ocp node add --gpu` writes `spec.gpu` as a JSON boolean (karr #50).
      The CRD declares it boolean and rejected the Perl integer with a
      422, so the flag could not be used at all — the CR was never
      written. Note that nothing reads the field yet (karr #70).
    - `ocp node add --nowait` replaces `--no_wait`; the old spelling keeps
      working as an alias (karr #37). `--no-wait` was never possible:
      MooX::Options strips a literal `no-` as a negation marker before it
      substitutes dashes for underscores, so it was read as "negate the
      option `wait`", which does not exist. Negative flags in this CLI are
      therefore spelled without the dash, like the existing `--nogit` and
      `--nopassword`.
    - `ocp deploy-image` accepts `--no-wait` and `--no-restart`. Both
      flags were declared `is_bool => 1`, a key MooX::Options does not
      know and silently ignores, so `--restart` (on by default) had no
      working way to turn it off at all — despite the POD, the help text
      and the runtime message all promising `--no_restart`. Defaults are
      unchanged: do not wait, do restart.
    - `ocp init --hetzner` now asks for the location and the server type
      instead of silently writing the hardcoded cpx21/fsn1 defaults (karr
      #27). The catalogue comes from the Hetzner API with the token that
      was just entered, pressing Enter keeps the previous default, and a
      run without a TTY, without a token or with an unreachable catalogue
      stays on the defaults instead of blocking.
    - The GPU Operator's ClusterPolicy no longer sets
      `CONTAINERD_SET_AS_DEFAULT` or `CONTAINERD_RUNTIME_CLASS` (karr
      #30). Both come from the archived 22.9-era recipe.
      `CONTAINERD_RUNTIME_CLASS` is overwritten by the operator before it
      reaches the DaemonSet, and `CONTAINERD_SET_AS_DEFAULT=1` asked for
      the opposite of OCP's own decision not to make the NVIDIA runtime
      the default handler for every container on the node — it stood
      still only because CDI outvotes it. `CONTAINERD_CONFIG` and
      `CONTAINERD_SOCKET` stay; they are the input the operator derives
      its runtime paths and mounts from. This changes the manifest hash,
      so the next `ocp apply` on a GPU cluster redeploys the GPU stack.
    - robocop may delete Kubernetes Nodes (karr #35). `OCP::Node::teardown`
      deletes the Node object, but the ClusterRole granted only get and
      patch, so under robocop the call would have failed with a 403 inside
      an eval that discarded the answer — the CR gone, the Node left
      behind as NotReady, teardown reporting success. A failing delete now
      warns and names the object; an already-absent object stays quiet.
    - A new Rex task removes the obsolete containerd `config.toml.tmpl`
      that OCP wrote before it stopped configuring containerd (karr #45).
      RKE2 and k3s render their config from such a template instead of
      their own generated one on every service start, so a host
      bootstrapped by an older OCP kept running from a two-line stub
      indefinitely. The file is removed only when its content is exactly
      that stub — a template someone wrote themselves is left alone —
      and the cleanup runs on bootstrap and on agent installs, not on the
      reconcile path.
    - `ocp apply --dry-run` no longer writes to a cluster that already
      exists (karr #52). The flag was checked in exactly one place, and
      that place sat behind the "cluster already exists" branch, so a
      reconcile ran every server-side apply, every CRD ensure and every
      Rex remedy a real run runs — harmless only because those writes
      are idempotent. A dry run now stops before the first write, prints
      the drift it found (with the remedy each entry would trigger) and
      ends with the same `[Dry run - no changes made]` line the bootstrap
      path already printed. It stamps no version and gives no health
      verdict, because it changed nothing.
    - `ocp status` now reports drift for NFD and the GPU Operator (karr
      #32). Both are pinned in OCP::Versions and were never compared
      against the cluster, so a version bump was rolled out silently and
      never named. Neither carries a Rex remedy — their version lives in
      a manifest `ocp apply` regenerates — so reconcile reports them
      without pointing at `ocp update`. A missing GPU Operator is not
      drift: it only belongs on a cluster with an NVIDIA card. The rest
      of the GPU pins (toolkit, device plugin, DCGM and its exporter,
      driver) stay unprobed on purpose; they live in the ClusterPolicy,
      not in a workload OCP writes.
    - The GPU Operator now reports what actually happened during
      reconcile instead of guessing from `.ocp/deployed.yaml` (karr #46).
      An operator that had gone missing from the cluster and was rolled
      out again at an unchanged manifest hash used to be announced as
      "up to date"; it now says "redeployed (was gone from the cluster)",
      alongside the deployed/updated/unchanged wording registry and NFD
      already used, plus "skipped" for a cluster with no GPU.
    - `ocp deploy-image` now accepts a single `--image REF` shorthand in
      addition to the existing `--repo` + `--tag` pair (karr #66). The
      shorthand parses either `REPO:TAG` (e.g. `ghcr.io/foo/bar:v1.2.3`,
      with implicit Docker Hub for the bare `foo/bar:v1.2.3` form) or the
      digest form `REPO@sha256:<64hex>` for content-addressable rollouts.
      `--image` is mutually exclusive with both `--repo` and `--tag`; all
      three fail loud when combined so the caller can't end up with two
      conflicting values. A bare `REPO` without a tag or digest still
      rejects loudly — there is no implicit `latest`, because pinned
      deploys want a name and a number, not a moving target. The parser
      is exposed as `_parse_image_ref` (callable as a class method) and
      is covered by `t/54-deploy-image.t` (parser + mutual exclusion +
      patch integration subtests).
    - New `ocp deploy-image` subcommand to roll out a new robocop image
      into the running cluster without `kubectl` (ADR 0007). Patches the
      robocop Deployment's controller container by name (strategic merge)
      and, by default, writes the `kubectl.kubernetes.io/restartedAt`
      annotation that `kubectl rollout restart` writes on the wire.
      Options: `--tag` (required, combined with `--repo` as REPO:TAG),
      `--repo` / `OCP_IMAGE_REPO` env (default `raudssus/ocp`),
      `--cluster` / `OCP_CLUSTER` env, `--namespace` (default
      `ocp-system`), `--wait` with `--timeout` (default 300s, polls on
      `availableReplicas` + `observedGeneration` vs `generation`),
      `--no_restart` to update the image without rolling the pods.
      Exits 0 on success, 1 on a wait timeout, fails loud on missing
      project, missing kubeconfig, missing Deployment, or cluster
      mismatch. `MooX::Cmd` derives the class name to `deployimage` and
      a `deploy-image` alias is registered in `OCP.pm` for the readable
      spelling. See `OCP::Cmd::DeployImage` POD and `t/54-deploy-image.t`.
    - `share/bin/ocp-build-image`, `make build-image`, `make docker-push`
      and `make docker-release` build a single image for the architecture
      of the machine running them. The Makefile's `PLATFORMS`,
      `buildx-setup` and `build-multiarch` targets are gone; `docker-push`
      and `docker-release` now build with `make build` (already a plain
      `docker build`, unchanged) and push each tag separately.
      `share/bin/ocp-build-image` no longer takes `--platforms` or
      `--builder`; it runs one plain `docker build` with its three tags
      plus `--cache-from`, then pushes each tag with its own `docker push`.
      Build and push are two separate commands now, so exit code 1
      (build) and 2 (push) name the command that actually failed instead
      of being recovered from a captured error stream.
    - New `share/bin/ocp-build-image` script and `make build-image`
      target for building and pushing `raudssus/ocp` images (karr #64).
      The script runs standalone (no `make`, no Perl, no network) and
      is idempotent / CI-friendly. Builds for the architecture of the
      machine running it, tags `latest` + `$VERSION` (from `./VERSION`
      or `git describe`) + 7-char git sha, pulls
      `--cache-from type=registry,ref=$REPO:cache` for incremental
      builds, and verifies `docker login` before pushing. `--repo` /
      `OCP_IMAGE_REPO` env overrides the repo, `--tag` overrides
      VERSION, `--no-push` builds locally, and `--dry-run` prints every
      docker command instead of executing it (the form the test suite
      uses). Exit codes: 0 success, 1 build fail, 2 push fail, 3 no
      docker login. `make build-image` wraps
      `share/bin/ocp-build-image --push`; it does NOT need maintainer
      go-ahead because the maintainer-approval gate that protects the
      older `docker-push`/`docker-release` Makefile targets no longer
      applies when CI calls the script directly. See
      `t/53-ocp-build-image.t`.
    - Decompose OCP::Cmd::Apply into focused phase modules
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::DeployedHash owns the .ocp/deployed.yaml
        tracking (load/save/report_component)
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::K8s owns the in-cluster K8s helpers
        (api/server_side_apply/apply_yaml_string/poll_deployment_ready/...)
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::Health owns the post-deploy cluster health
        gate (classify_pod/scan_pods/check/print/is_fatal/banner)
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::CR owns the OCPNode/OCPNodeProvider layer:
        CRD apply, provider CRs, CP+worker CRs, legacy migration,
        robocop deployment, robocop-ready wait, and the worker
        reconcile loop (CLI fallback + robocop poll). All twelve
        helpers re-exported from Apply.pm as thin forwarders so the
        test surface (t/20, t/36, t/38) keeps working
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::Registry owns the docker.io pull-through
        cache + local registry (manifest generation, deployment+NodePort
        shape, the up-to-date check that also asks the cluster, and the
        ocpVersion status stamp). Helpers re-exported from Apply.pm
        including deployment/nodeport_service which t/33 calls as bare
        package subs
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::Workloads owns the opt-in cluster workloads
        (NFD setup + manifest + GPU operator setup + ClusterPolicy
        manifest). All five helpers re-exported from Apply.pm as thin
        forwarders so the test surface (t/40-gpu-clusterpolicy.t)
        keeps working without changes
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::Network owns the cluster ingress path:
        cert-manager (downstream apply + the selfsigned/ACME issuer
        CRs with their retry-around-webhook loop), the Cilium Gateway,
        LB-IPAM (CiliumLoadBalancerIPPool + L2AnnouncementPolicy),
        and the CoreDNS/registry.local machinery (the writers and the
        pure-functional Corefile helpers). The @COREDNS_CONFIGMAPS
        array re-exported from Apply.pm so t/39's writer/drift-probe
        consistency check still works. All eleven helpers
        re-exported from Apply.pm as thin forwarders so t/31 and t/39
        keep working without changes
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::Drift owns the "cluster already exists"
        reconcile path (the existing-cluster branch of execute):
        OCP::Drift run + remedy dispatch, and the per-component
        convergence loop (registry, CoreDNS/registry.local, NFD,
        GPU operator, cert-manager, Cilium Gateway, LB-IPAM, and
        the CR layer — CRDs, provider CRs, CP OCPNode). Both
        _reconcile_components and _run_remedy re-exported from
        Apply.pm as thin forwarders so t/20 and t/38 keep working.
        t/38's regex-on-source now reads Drift.pm for the body of
        reconcile_components; t/47's hash-reader scan concatenates
        Apply.pm + Drift.pm so the invariant "whoever reads the
        hashes also asks the cluster" still spans the two files
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::Bootstrap owns the first-deploy server-side
        sequence (server provision + SSH wait, Rex install_server,
        encrypted kubeconfig save, wait-for-Ready / Cilium CNI) plus
        the spec-shaped identity helpers (cp_identity, dist_label)
        and the PIN2-driven SSH key prep (setup_ssh_key). The whole
        sequence is one call from execute(): bootstrap_control_plane
        returns a hashref with the api handle, cp_name, cp_ip,
        provider and ssh_key_path. _cp_identity, _dist_label and
        _setup_ssh_key re-exported from Apply.pm as thin forwarders
        so t/37 (dist_label) and t/38 (cp_identity) keep working.
        t/28's "three places answer which distribution version" was
        updated: the installer entry is now Bootstrap.pm instead of
        Apply.pm (the regex pattern is unchanged)
      - OCP::Cmd::Apply::Deploy owns the post-bootstrap half of the
        fresh-deploy path: registry + CoreDNS/registry.local, NFD +
        GPU operator, cert-manager apply + wait + issuers, Cilium
        Gateway, LB-IPAM, the CR layer (CRDs/providers/CP OCPNode),
        worker OCPNodes + robocop rollout + CLI reconcile fallback.
        execute() now reads as a dispatcher: config load → reconcile
        or auth → bootstrap_control_plane → deploy → finish_apply.
        _finish_apply moved to OCP::Cmd::Apply::Health::finish (still
        re-exported as a thin forwarder for the test surface)
      - t/52-apply-dispatcher.t: the dispatcher test. Locks in the
        seam that keeps execute() a dispatcher — execute must
        delegate to the four phase modules (Drift, Bootstrap, Deploy,
        Health), the forwarders must actually delegate (not inline
        the implementation), execute must not own any component setup
        call, and the body size must stay under the budget. The
        budget will trip the next time someone tries to grow the
        dispatcher instead of growing a phase module
    - Make the OCP::Provider seam real
      - OCP::Provider had two parallel dispatch paths (`for_spec` and
        `from_cr`), each maintaining its own `if $type eq ...` ladder and
        its own error string. Adding a provider meant editing both. They
        now share one `_build(\%args)` dispatcher; the two entry points
        reduce to "gather credentials, then call _build". The previously
        divergent "Unsupported provider type" / "Unsupported provider"
        strings are unified into a single message; t/12-provider.t is
        updated to match
      - OCP::Role::Provider::ExistingHost had methods but no inline POD,
        so the contract had to be inferred from the body. Each method,
        the `verbose` attribute, and the three required hooks now carry
        `=attr` / `=method` blocks; `=description` names the rule
        ("Any adapter that wraps a host the user already controls MUST
        consume this role") and the seam it protects
      - OCP::Provider::Hetzner and the two host adapters (SSH, Local)
        get the same treatment: every method documented inline, the
        hash shapes they return called out (`{ id, ip, newly_created }`
        for create, `{ stdout, stderr, exit }` for run), and the
        idempotency contract on create_server spelled out so the next
        adapter knows the rules without grepping the call sites
    - Three new tests pin the seam down
      - t/49-provider-factory.t: dispatch matrix for both entry points
        (spec/cr × ssh/local/hetzner), unified error string, missing
        tokenSecretRef handled, typed Kind args (no `path =>`)
      - t/50-existing-host-role.t: composition requires the three
        transport hooks, the no-op trio returns as documented, the
        hash shapes match, delete_server actually runs the uninstall
        command
      - t/51-pod.t: every Provider*.pm and Role/Provider*.pm has
        `# ABSTRACT`, `=synopsis`, `=description`, `=seealso`, and
        inline `=method` for each public method — no manual `=head1`
        NAME/AUTHOR/SUPPORT/COPYRIGHT (PodWeaver builds those)
    - Name the Hetzner pickers for what they are
      - OCP::Hetzner → OCP::Hetzner::Picker; the class wraps the read-only
        catalogue (locations, server types) and has nothing to do with
        creating or deleting servers, which is OCP::Provider::Hetzner's
        job
      - the `use` in OCP::Cmd::Init is updated; the picker tests under
        t/42-hetzner-options.t exercise the new name
      - who-shares-what is now obvious from the namespace: ::Picker for
        the catalogue wrapper, ::Provider for the cluster adapter
    - Make `ocp hetzner` a subcommand group, with `list` underneath
      - the previous flat `OCP::Cmd::Hetzner` was the listing command
        itself, which read as if `ocp hetzner` would talk to the
        cluster adapter. It never did — it talks to the Hetzner Cloud
        API directly via WWW::Hetzner::Cloud — but the name did not
        say so
      - OCP::Cmd::Hetzner is now a dispatch group (like `ocp node` and
        `ocp provider`); OCP::Cmd::Hetzner::List owns the listing
      - the no-op `--list` flag is gone — the verb IS the subcommand
      - `ocp hetzner` now asks for a subcommand, `ocp hetzner list` is
        the listing; t/26-cli-dispatch.t exercises both
    - Drop OCP::Cmd::InjectKey
      - sub execute died with a TODO message instead of doing anything;
        reimplementation is tracked on the board (karr #2) and will land as
        a fresh command, not a resurrection of the dead one
      - the 'inject-key' alias and the corresponding entry in bin/ocp's help
        and the OCP.pm POD list went with it; the WORKFLOW section in
        OCP::Robocop now points at karr #2 instead
    - Take share/ from the tree the code came from, not from the image
      - OCP::Rex and both share-dir lookups in the command modules searched
        /opt/ocp/src/share first, so inside a container the image always won
        — including over a working tree mounted next to it. The usual
        `-v $REPO:/src -I/src/lib /src/bin/ocp` run therefore executed the
        modules from the tree and the Rexfile from the last built image; an
        arm64 bootstrap pulled the amd64 tarball because the fix for that was
        in the tree only
      - one resolver (OCP::Share), one order: the share directory next to the
        running script, then the image path, then File::ShareDir. In the image
        that is the same directory as before. OCP_SHARE_DIR overrides all of
        it and fails loudly rather than falling through
      - Rex now runs from a private copy of the Rexfile, because it writes its
        lock file next to it and a read-only share/ (a :ro mount, a root-owned
        install) killed the run before the first task
      - xt/smoke.sh passes the working tree's share/ explicitly, which is what
        its header comment always claimed it did
    - Let `ocp destroy` take .ocp/deployed.yaml with it
      - the file records the manifest hashes of the components last rolled
        out; surviving a destroy, it made the next apply skip components on a
        cluster built from scratch — "Registry already deployed (up to date)"
        over an empty ocp-system, with CoreDNS pointed at a registry that did
        not exist
      - the registry setup now asks the cluster before claiming to be up to
        date, as NFD, the GPU operator and cert-manager already did, and
        reconcile reports what actually happened instead of deciding a second
        time from the file
    - Fix worker provisioning, which could never have worked against a real
      cluster
      - OCP::Node addressed the Kubernetes API with an api-version in the
        first argument — `get('ocp.internal/v1', 'OCPNode', ...)`,
        `delete('v1', 'Node', ...)` — a form Kubernetes::REST does not have:
        argument 0 is the Kind and is resolved through expand_class(), so
        those calls died rather than being merely misrouted
      - `update()` was handed the CR as a plain hash, which dies on
        `->metadata`; both lease writes went through it
      - since _acquire_lease is the first thing _provision does, every worker
        went straight to phase=Failed; both teardown deletes sit inside eval
        blocks, so teardown reported success while leaving the Kubernetes Node
        object and the OCPNode CR behind
      - the reconcile dispatch sent phase=Installing (what _provision writes)
        to _wait_ready, leaving the agent install reachable only from
        phase=Provisioning, which nothing in OCP ever writes: the RKE2 agent
        was never installed and the node waited for a registration that could
        not happen
      - _release_lease now re-reads before writing, so the resourceVersion
        bumped by the intervening /status patch cannot 409 the release and
        strand the lease until its TTL expired
      - a read that fails for any reason other than 404 no longer counts as
        "no CR here": the lease is not taken on the strength of a CR nobody
        could read, while a blip during the poll loop stays survivable
      - releasing the lease is best-effort, so a provisioned machine is not
        marked Failed — which is terminal — because the release call failed;
        the lease then simply stands until its TTL and the same holder takes
        it again
      - the CLI worker path converts the OCPNodeProvider CR before handing it
        to OCP::Provider->from_cr, which reads it as a plain hash; it had been
        passing the typed object and relying on IO::K8s objects happening to
        be blessed hashes
    - Give robocop a Kubernetes client it can actually build
      - OCP::Robocop::Controller built its client with
        `Kubernetes::REST->new(kubeconfig => ...)`, or with nothing at all on
        the assumption that the client picks up in-cluster config by itself;
        Kubernetes::REST has neither a `kubeconfig` argument nor an in-cluster
        automatism, so both branches died with "Missing required arguments:
        credentials, server" — and since `kube` is what the first line of the
        reconcile loop touches, the controller could not survive its own first
        iteration
      - the client now comes from OCP::Kubernetes, which is how the CLI
        already reaches a cluster, extended with an explicit `in_cluster` mode
        for the pod service account; it is opt-in, so a caller that lost its
        kubeconfig still fails with the message that says so instead of
        quietly authenticating as something else
      - a kubeconfig handed over as content is no longer fed to `-f`, which
        warned about a filename containing newlines on its way to answering
        false
    - Drive the OCPNode tests through a real Kubernetes::REST
      - t/16-node.t used a hand-written double that accepted every signature
        and returned the same canned CR, which is why all of the above stayed
        green; it now runs the shipped client over a mock transport, so a
        wrong call dies in the test for the same reason it dies in production,
        and t/43-k8s-call-contract.t pins the contract the double relies on
    - Let a bare `ocp init` scaffold a project
      - the provider defaulted to ssh while `--help` advertised hetzner, so
        `ocp init` with no flags died on a missing --host instead of writing
        an ocp.yaml — hetzner is now the default here too, matching what an
        absent provider already means everywhere in OCP::Config
      - init stays offline: it warns that a Hetzner token is still missing
        rather than prompting, `ocp init --hetzner` remains the way to store
        one, and `--provider ssh` without --host now also names the default
    - Drop `ocp init --single`
      - the option was declared and read nowhere; a fresh init already writes
        one control plane and no workers, which is exactly what
        OCP::Config::single_node derives, so there was nothing to switch
    - Stop putting .kube/ in the generated .gitignore
      - no code path writes a project-local kubeconfig any more —
        `ocp kubeconfig -e` merges into $KUBECONFIG or ~/.kube/config
      - .ocp/ is now the only entry OCP adds; the encrypted files
        (keys.yaml, secrets.yaml, age.key.enc, kubeconfig.yaml) are meant to
        be committed, and the tests now assert git would not swallow them
    - Show Hetzner's ARM server types
      - OCP::Hetzner::server_type_options filtered the catalogue to x86, so
        the CAX line could never be offered; both architectures are listed
        now, each label names its own, and `architecture` remains available
        as a deliberate filter
    - Stop taking cluster DNS down on k3s
      - CoreDNS allows the hosts plugin once per server block; k3s already
        runs it for /etc/coredns/NodeHosts, so the block `ocp apply` spliced
        in for registry.local was a second one and CoreDNS crash-looped with
        "plugin/hosts: this plugin can only be used once per Server Block"
      - the record now joins the hosts plugin the distribution already has
        and only gets a block of its own where there is none (RKE2), and a
        control plane that moved updates the address instead of being skipped
      - the ConfigMap is also looked up under the name RKE2 gives it
        (rke2-coredns-rke2-coredns), where the whole step used to be a
        silent no-op
    - Pull the GPU validator from an image that exists
      - the standalone gpu-operator-validator repository stops at v25.3.4,
        so the pinned v26.3.3 404ed and every GPU DaemonSet sat in
        Init:ImagePullBackOff — the validator is their init container
      - since v25.10 the operator image ships the validator itself, which is
        what the ClusterPolicy now names; the separate pin is gone, it can
        only drift away from the operator version
    - Write OCPNode status where Kubernetes actually keeps it
      - the OCPNode CRD enables the status subresource, and the API server
        silently drops the status of every write to the main endpoint —
        answering 2xx, so nothing looked wrong
      - `ocp apply` therefore stored control-plane CRs with no status at
        all: `ocp node ls` showed a Ready control plane as Pending with an
        empty IP while `ocp status` showed it Ready with its address
      - the same applied to every worker phase transition, so a node's
        phase never outlived the process that set it
      - status now goes through /status, and apply no longer prints
        "(control-plane, Ready)" when the write did not land
      - the control plane's IP is read off the Node object instead of the
        configured host, which is frequently a DNS name
    - Make `ocp apply` reconcile an existing cluster, not just report on it
      - applying to a cluster that already had a kubeconfig took a second
        path that returned before the health gate, so it printed component
        results and exited 0 without ever looking at the cluster
      - both paths now end in one shared finisher: one health verdict, one
        banner, one exit code
      - that path also never ran the steps that repair state OCP does not
        own. k3s' addon manager restores its own Corefile and drops the
        registry.local record with it, and nothing put it back — a cluster
        whose DNS entry k3s removed stayed broken however often apply ran
      - it now reconciles registry.local DNS, the Cilium Gateway, LB-IPAM
        (when enabled), the CRDs, the provider CRs and the control-plane
        OCPNode, so clusters bootstrapped by an older OCP stop showing
        their control plane as Pending
      - provisioning stays out: creating servers and waiting on them is a
        bootstrap step, and this is the path people run often
    - Check the cluster before `ocp apply` claims success
      - apply reported CONTROL PLANE DEPLOYED SUCCESSFULLY! and exited 0
        while CoreDNS was in CrashLoopBackOff and five gpu-operator pods
        hung in ImagePullBackOff — a statement about the deploy, not the
        cluster
      - apply now waits for pods to settle and then reports what is still
        durably broken; pods that are merely starting are never a fault
      - a fault in kube-system fails the deploy, because DNS or CNI down
        means there is no working cluster; opt-in add-ons warn loudly and
        leave the exit code alone
    - Name the distribution being installed
      - a `dist: k3s` cluster announced "Installing RKE2 server..." while
        install_k3s_server was the task running
    - Record the OCP version at the end of `ocp apply`
      - `ocp update` refuses to run without status.ocpVersion and `ocp
        version` cannot name what is deployed without it, but the only
        writer was update itself, at the end of a run it never reached
      - on a cluster this CLI had just bootstrapped, `ocp update` answered
        "Cluster not yet deployed" while `ocp status` listed a Ready node
      - update also tells the two situations apart now instead of sending
        every caller to `ocp apply first`
    - Add `make smoke` — a full bootstrap against a real machine
      - init, apply, status, kubeconfig, node ls, update --dry-run, destroy,
        asserting on each; runs the working tree inside the image
      - nothing in t/ can execute OCP, which is why a k3s join on the wrong
        port, a cluster named "." and a crash-looping registry all shipped
        green
      - needs SMOKE_HOST and wipes the cluster there, so there is no default
    - Stop ocp-cache from crash-looping its way to readiness
      - registry:2 in pull-through mode resolves its upstream once, while
        starting, and panics when DNS does not answer; on a fresh cluster
        CoreDNS is regularly seconds behind, so the cache restarted three
        times before it stayed up
      - an init container now waits for the name to resolve, reusing the
        registry image so there is no second pull, and gives up after 2min
      - both registries also carry a readiness probe on /v2/; without one a
        Deployment counts as ready the moment the container starts, which is
        what ocp apply believed before pointing containerd at the cache
    - Take the agent registration port from the distribution
      - both join sites built "https://$cp_ip:9345", the RKE2 supervisor
        port; k3s serves joins from 6443, so a k3s worker was pointed at a
        port nothing listens on
      - OCP::Config->join_url and ->supervisor_port answer this in one place
    - Stop advertising the join port as the API endpoint
      - the closing banner of `ocp apply` printed :9345, while the
        kubeconfig it had just written pointed at :6443
    - Fix `ocp status` naming a provider twice
      - "Control Planes: 1 (ssh, ssh)": the server type fell back to 'ssh'
        when a provider has none, and was printed next to the provider
      - the node table also cut into the version and roles columns, which
        no longer fit a name like v1.36.3+rke2r1 or control-plane,etcd
    - Fix the cluster name defaulting to "." instead of the directory name
      - ocp init took it from Path::Tiny's path('.')->basename, which is
        literally "."; the name landed in ocp.yaml and from there in node
        labels and the Hetzner cluster tag
      - under Docker the project is mounted at /ocp, so the directory name
        is always "ocp" there: pass --name for anything else
    - Rename the ocp.yaml keys to snake_case and drop the legacy aliases
      - controlPlanes/cps -> control_planes, serverType -> server_type,
        k8s -> kubernetes, ssh.privateKey/publicKey -> ssh.private_key and
        ssh.public_key, publicIp -> public_ip
      - the deprecation warnings for `cps` and `k8s` went with them, so an
        ocp.yaml written before this has to be converted by hand
      - `nodes: cp` is no longer accepted, `nodes: 1` says the same thing
      - the CRD fields keep their camelCase names (serverType, publicIP,
        providerRef): those are Kubernetes API objects, and a structural
        schema prunes every field it does not declare
    - Fix Rex task parameters never reaching the Rexfile
      - OCP::Rex encoded them as JSON into REX_TASK_PARAMS, but no task ever
        read that variable, so every parameter silently vanished and
        upgrade_cilium died with "version parameter required"
      - tasks now read them through task_params() in the Rexfile; Rex's own
        --key=value form still wins, for running a task by hand
      - keeps the join token out of the process command line, where ps
        exposes it to every local user
    - Resolve the distribution version from OCP::Versions when ocp.yaml does
      not pin one
      - ocp apply fell back to '', which made the Rex task install the
        distribution's stable channel, while OCP::Drift and OCP::Node both
        answered from the version manifest
      - a fresh cluster was therefore reported as drifted against OCP's own
        manifest, and workers joined one minor ahead of the apiserver
    - Pass the Cilium, Cilium CLI and Gateway API versions to install_cilium
      - the Rexfile constants had drifted behind the version manifest, so a
        bootstrap installed Cilium 1.19.2 where 1.20.0 was pinned
      - Gateway API is version-locked to Cilium and is now pinned alongside
        it; 1.20 requires the v1.6.1 CRDs and refuses to start its Gateway
        controller against older bundles
    - Disable rke2-traefik and rke2-traefik-crd
      - RKE2 v1.36 added Traefik, whose helm-install job CrashLoopBackOffs on
        a Cilium-owned cluster
    - Fix ocp apply and ocp destroy exiting 1 on success
      - run_cli uses execute()'s return value as the exit code, and a
        trailing print() evaluates to 1
    - Check the HTTP status of every server-side apply
      - _request is the raw transport and never inspects the response, so
        failed applies were silent: a 404 looked exactly like success
      - this also broke the cert-manager issuer retry, which treated the
        first attempt as successful and never retried
    - Report the cert-manager issuers that were actually created
      - the planned list was printed unconditionally while failures went to
        stderr, so a run that created nothing still looked clean
    - Apply CRDs as raw manifests instead of through the typed ensure()
      - a CRD schema is full of union-typed fields that IO::K8s cannot
        inflate, which killed ocp apply on a clean bootstrap
    - ocp destroy removes what OCP installed on top of the distribution
      - the vendor uninstallers leave the Cilium CLI, /opt/cni and /run/k3s
        behind, and a stale CLI was adopted by the next bootstrap
    - ocp status shows the distribution version that would be installed
      instead of the word 'latest'
    - Give every file under lib/ and bin/ its own $VERSION
    - Use JSON::MaybeXS for JSON encoding and decoding
    - Drift detection (OCP::Drift)
      - compares running component versions against the version manifest
      - compares kubelet versions against the configured distribution
      - compares publicIp pinned in ocp.yaml against the recorded status
      - ocp status reports drift, ocp apply runs the upgrade step for what
        can be fixed automatically instead of dying
    - ocp kubeconfig -e merges into $KUBECONFIG or ~/.kube/config
      - renames cluster/user/context from 'default' to the cluster name
      - keeps entries of other clusters, backs up the previous file
      - apply no longer writes a plaintext .kube/config into the project
    - Clean error messages: no Perl stacktraces, --verbose for the raw error
    - Fix ocp version and ocp update: OCP::Config was missing status,
      cluster_status, set_status and save_status
    - Fix ocp node/provider subcommands: MooX::Cmd looked for them under
      ::Cmd, so 'ocp node ls' never dispatched
    - Fix ocp update option specs (format => '' is not valid)
    - Accept 'ocp deploy-robocop' and 'ocp inject-key' spellings
    - Accept a positional NAME for ocp node add/rm
    - Fix OCP::Node->teardown passing the node name where the provider id
      belongs, which left Hetzner servers running after ocp node rm
    - Share provider behaviour via OCP::Role::Provider::ExistingHost; the
      local provider now runs its commands without SSH
    - Remove Traefik leftovers (replaced by Cilium Gateway API)
    - Remove Tickit/TUI leftovers from Dockerfile and cpanfile
    - kubectl is back in the Docker image as a debugging tool
    - Add shared OCP::Kubernetes typed helper for cluster access
      - centralizes Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig setup for OCP commands
      - provides reusable node summary helpers for typed Node objects
      - keeps optional IO::K8s provider registration in one place
    - Refactor ocp status to use typed Kubernetes API access
      - removes kubectl as the primary status data path
      - formats node summaries via shared Perl helpers
      - improves groundwork for moving more commands off shell-driven K8s access
    - Core Registry: Pull-through cache (docker.io) + local registry
      - ocp-cache (NodePort 30500) caches all Docker Hub pulls
      - ocp-registry (NodePort 30501) for user images
      - RKE2 registries.yaml auto-configured on all nodes
      - Deployed first after node Ready (all subsequent pulls cached)
    - Component reconciliation with hash tracking
      - ocp apply on existing clusters checks/deploys missing components
      - .ocp/deployed.yaml tracks MD5 of deployed manifests
      - Changed manifests (new OCP version) trigger re-deploy
    - Complete NVIDIA GPU support for Kubernetes
      - Unified install_nvidia task (replaces separate Hetzner/generic)
      - All required packages: nvidia-driver, nvidia-smi, libcuda1, nvidia-container-toolkit
      - Kernel headers for running kernel (prevents DKMS mismatch)
      - Verification: module loaded, nvidia-smi, libcuda, container-toolkit
      - containerd nvidia runtime configured before RKE2 start (no restart needed)
      - RuntimeClass + Device Plugin auto-deployed by ocp apply
      - GPU pods work with runtimeClassName: nvidia + nvidia.com/gpu resource
    - Cilium Gateway API (replaces Traefik)
      - Gateway with HTTP/HTTPS listeners
      - LB-IPAM for bare-metal LoadBalancer support
      - CiliumL2AnnouncementPolicy for external IP
    - Fix SSH host key verification (switch from Net::SSH2 to Net::OpenSSH)
      - Net::SSH2 (libssh2) silently ignores StrictHostKeyChecking setting
      - Unknown hosts no longer cause "Couldn't authenticate" errors
    - AMD GPU: Disabled for now (ROCm not available on Debian)
      - AMD dropped Debian from ROCm packages (only Ubuntu/RHEL/SLES)
      - ROCm userspace belongs in container images, not on host
      - detect_gpu still detects AMD but skips driver installation
    - RKE2 + K3s dual distribution support via Rex framework
    - Cilium CNI installation (kube-proxy replacement, eBPF)
    - Robust GPU detection with CUDA compute capability check
      - PCI class codes [0300]/[0302] + vendor IDs [10de]/[1002]
      - NVIDIA compute check: only installs for CUDA-capable GPUs
      - Whitelisted: RTX, GTX 10xx+, Quadro, Tesla, datacenter (A/H/L/V/P)
      - Skips: GeForce MX (laptop), GT/GTS/NVS (legacy), pre-Pascal
      - Virtual GPU detection (virtio/QEMU/VMware/VirtualBox)
    - Single-node mode (control plane untainting)
    - Rex-based server automation (share/Rexfile)
    - Robocop Kubernetes controller for CRD-based node management
      - OCPNode and OCPNodeProvider CRDs
      - Net::Async::Kubernetes based reconciliation loop
      - In-cluster controller deployment
    - Development registry support (ocp dev command)
      - Build robocop image from source
      - In-cluster registry deployment
      - Auto-update controller deployments
    - Local provider with auto-detection
      - Docker mode: SSH to localhost with clear messaging
      - Native mode: Direct installation via OCP::Local
      - Automatic environment detection (/.dockerenv)
    - CLI commands: init, apply, status, destroy, kubeconfig, hetzner, dev
    - Hetzner Cloud provider support via WWW::Hetzner
    - SSH provider for existing servers (control planes + workers)
    - Pure Perl secrets management via Crypt::Age and File::SOPS
    - Spec/Status separation (ocp.yaml vs .ocp/status.yaml)
    - Age encryption for secrets (no external tools required)
    - SSH key generation and management
    - Clean error messages (no Perl stacktraces)
